# @PROJECT_NAME@

Generated Qornix Web RAG application.

This project is created by:

```bash
./create_new_project.sh ../@PROJECT_NAME@ --template rag_app
```

It embeds the reusable `qornix_rag` module into a normal Qornix Web application. It does not use the standalone-only `qornix_rag/run.sh` launcher.

## Build

```bash
mkdir -p build
cd build
cmake ..
cmake --build .
```

## Run

From the build directory:

```bash
./@PROJECT_NAME@
```

Open:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8008/rag
http://127.0.0.1:8008/api/rag/health
```

## RAG Endpoints

```text
GET  /rag
GET  /api/rag/health
GET  /api/rag/admin/diagnostics
GET  /api/rag/metrics
POST /api/rag/ask
POST /api/rag/search
GET  /api/rag/sources
POST /api/rag/index
GET  /api/rag/qa
POST /api/rag/qa
PUT  /api/rag/qa/{id}
DEL  /api/rag/qa/{id}
```

Compatibility QA routes are also available as `/api/rag/qa/list`,
`/api/rag/qa/add`, `/api/rag/qa/update`, and `/api/rag/qa/delete`.

## Local LLM

The default config expects Ollama:

```bash
ollama serve
ollama pull llama3.2:3b
```

Change the model or API endpoint in `config.yaml`:

```yaml
rag:
  llm:
    enabled: true
    api_url: http://localhost:11434
    model: llama3.2:3b
```

Set `rag.llm.enabled: false` for search-only mode.

## Knowledge Base

The template enables:

- SQLite QA storage at `data/rag_kb.db`;
- Markdown source indexing from `knowledge_base/`;
- optional embedding model files under `models/`;
- RAG UI at `/rag`;
- RAG API under `/api/rag/*`.

## Retrieval Models

You do not need an ONNX model for the first run. The default embedding backend is TF-IDF:

```yaml
rag:
  embedding:
    backend: tfidf
```

This mode works without model files. It uses lexical retrieval and is the safest default.

ONNX semantic retrieval is optional. It can improve meaning-based matching, but the current loader expects a compatible BERT-style text embedding model. It needs:

- `models/semantic_model.onnx`;
- `models/tokenizer.json`;
- a tokenizer with `model.vocab`;
- ONNX inputs such as `input_ids`, `attention_mask`, and optionally `token_type_ids`;
- a float output shaped `[1, sequence_length, hidden_size]` or `[1, hidden_size]`.

To enable it, put compatible files under `models/`:

```text
models/
  semantic_model.onnx
  tokenizer.json
```

Recommended one-command setup:

```bash
./download_onnx_model.sh
```

The script downloads the default BERT-style ONNX text embedding model from Hugging Face, saves it under `models/`, and updates this app's `config.yaml` to `rag.embedding.backend: onnx`.

## Operations

See `doc/operations.md` for Docker Compose, persistent volumes, diagnostics, metrics, rate limits, backup/restore, and network exposure guidance.

Generated full web apps can use `qornix_auth` with a `qornix_orm`-backed store. Set `auth.enabled: true`, configure `auth.database` for SQLite/PostgreSQL/MySQL, and bootstrap or register an admin user before exposing the app. The older `rag.security` token/header guard remains available as a lightweight fallback for simple deployments.

Useful options:

```bash
./download_onnx_model.sh --help
./download_onnx_model.sh --dry-run
./download_onnx_model.sh --force
./download_onnx_model.sh --no-config-update
```

You can get those files in two common ways.

Download an existing ONNX export from Hugging Face Hub:

```bash
python3 -m pip install --user huggingface_hub
huggingface-cli download <model-repo-id> \
  --include "*.onnx" "tokenizer.json" \
  --local-dir models/downloaded
cp models/downloaded/*.onnx models/semantic_model.onnx
cp models/downloaded/tokenizer.json models/tokenizer.json
```

Or export a Hugging Face text encoder model yourself:

```bash
python3 -m pip install --user "optimum[onnxruntime]" transformers
optimum-cli export onnx \
  --model <model-repo-id> \
  --task feature-extraction \
  models/exported
cp models/exported/*.onnx models/semantic_model.onnx
cp models/exported/tokenizer.json models/tokenizer.json
```

Use a text encoder / embedding model. Do not use Ollama models, `.gguf` files,
chat LLM weights, image models, or arbitrary ONNX files here.

Official references:

- Hugging Face Hub: https://huggingface.co/models
- Optimum ONNX export: https://huggingface.co/docs/optimum/exporters/onnx/usage_guides/export_a_model
- ONNX Runtime install: https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/install/

Then update `config.yaml`:

```yaml
rag:
  embedding:
    backend: onnx
    model_path: models/semantic_model.onnx
    tokenizer_path: models/tokenizer.json
    max_seq_len: 512
    onnx_threads: 2
    normalize_embeddings: true
    enable_fallback: true
```

Restart the application and check:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8008/api/rag/health
```

Model files are not bundled by default because embedding model licensing and size vary. Keep `enable_fallback: true` while testing a new model so the app can continue with TF-IDF if ONNX cannot be initialized.

Add Markdown files to `knowledge_base/`, then call:

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8008/api/rag/index \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{}'
```

Ask:

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8008/api/rag/ask \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"question":"What is in this knowledge base?","top_k":5}'
```

## Deploy Bundle

Build the deploy target:

```bash
cmake --build build --target @PROJECT_NAME@_deploy
```

Run:

```bash
cd build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@
./@PROJECT_NAME@
```

The deploy bundle contains the executable, `config.yaml`, UI templates, static assets, docs, `knowledge_base/`, `data/`, and `logs/`.

## Framework Path

The generated `CMakeLists.txt` points to the Qornix Web checkout used by the generator. Override it if the framework moves:

```bash
cmake -DQORNIX_WEB_ROOT=/path/to/qornix_web ..
```
